<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Farrukhabad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Farrukhabad is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was founded in 1714 by the Afghan commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-khan-bangash">Muhammad Khan Bangash</a>, who named it after the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhsiyar">Farrukhsiyar</a>, and it became the seat of the Bangash nawabs. Ceded to the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in 1802, it was known for textile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> and trade, and with neighbouring Fatehgarh it forms a twin town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/farrukhabad.jpg" alt="Farrukhabad" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohammad Aqib Khan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Mohd._Khan_Bangash_Nawab.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
